{"absent":["Locking and memory-ordering rules for global CPU masks, mask variables, and concurrent per-CPU updates.","Definitions and lifecycles of configured maximum, live count, small scan width, large width, and NUMA node arguments.","Out-of-range and empty-result behavior for scans, ordinal selection, random choice, shifts, and singleton lookup beyond checked indices.","Error and truncation behavior of user and kernel string parsers and bounded print helpers.","Population, ownership, and consumption of crash reservation ranges and kexec image state.","Conditions selecting single-CPU versus multi-CPU views and static versus dynamic mask variable configurations."],"dossier":"D12","findings":[{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_first_and_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_last","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_zero","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_and_wrap","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_wrap","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_nth_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_nth_and_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_random"],"text":"Mask scanning reports positions of set or unset entries over a small configured width, covering earliest set in a triple intersection, latest set, successor set or unset after a start, successor under pairwise intersection or difference with wrapping and non-wrapping forms, ordinal selection under intersection combinations, and randomized selection bounded by the live CPU count."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_local_spread","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_and_distribute","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_distribute","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_but","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_and_but","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_andnot_but"],"text":"Single-choice selection reduces a scan to one representative CPU, with distribution variants that fall back to earliest-set semantics, a node spread helper that always yields the first CPU, simple aliases for earliest set and earliest intersection, and exclusion variants that return the first acceptable CPU different from a given one."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu_wrap","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu_or","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_cpu_from"],"text":"Iteration helpers walk set bits of one mask, optionally resuming from the current value or wrapping from a start, with combined forms walking pairwise intersection, difference, or union of two masks without materializing an intermediate result."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_set_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear_cpus","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_and_set_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_and_clear_cpu"],"text":"Per-CPU accessors test, set, or clear one position with checked indices, offering atomic test-and-modify forms and relaxed double-underscore forms, plus a range clear that validates the covered span before clearing."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_setall","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_copy","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_right","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_left","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_or","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weighted_or","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_xor","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_equal","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_or_equal","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_intersects","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_subset","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_empty","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_full","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight_andnot"],"text":"Whole-mask operations fill or empty a destination, copy between masks, shift contents by a distance, compute pairwise intersection union exclusive-or difference and weighted union with emptiness or count indication where shown, compare for equality or union-equality, test intersection or subset relations, and report population counts overall or under intersection or difference."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_parse_user","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_parselist_user","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_parse","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpulist_parse","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumap_print_bitmask_to_buf","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumap_print_list_to_buf","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_size"],"text":"String converters translate between textual mask or list representations and in-memory masks for user and kernel buffers with bounded destination width, print helpers render a mask as bitmask or list into a bounded buffer with offset and length handling, and a size helper reports allocation footprint for the large width."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::get_cpu_mask","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_of","include/linux/cpumask.h::to_cpumask","include/linux/cpumask.h::__check_is_bitmap"],"text":"Singleton mask access derives a read-only mask identifying exactly one CPU from a shared table using word-size arithmetic, with a convenience alias for that lookup and a cast helper that validates bitmap type at compile time."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::alloc_cpumask_var_node","include/linux/cpumask.h::alloc_cpumask_var.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::alloc_cpumask_var.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::zalloc_cpumask_var.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::zalloc_cpumask_var.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::zalloc_cpumask_var_node.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::zalloc_cpumask_var_node.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::alloc_bootmem_cpumask_var","include/linux/cpumask.h::free_cpumask_var","include/linux/cpumask.h::free_bootmem_cpumask_var","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_available.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_available.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::this_cpu_cpumask_var_ptr.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::this_cpu_cpumask_var_ptr.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_var_read_mostly.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_var_read_mostly.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPUMASK_VAR_NULL.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPUMASK_VAR_NULL.alt_2"],"text":"Mask variable management abstracts over configurable versus static allocation: one configuration allocates on a node with zeroing variants and null-ness availability checks plus per-CPU dereference, while the other treats allocation as always successful no-ops with clearing on zeroing allocation, empty boot and free operations, and constant availability."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_online_cpu.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_present_cpu.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_possible_cpu_wrap.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_online_cpu_wrap.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_online_cpu.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_enabled_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_present_cpu.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_possible_cpu_wrap.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::for_each_online_cpu_wrap.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_online_cpus.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_possible_cpus.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_enabled_cpus.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_present_cpus.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_active_cpus.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_online.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_enabled.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_possible.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_present.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_active.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_dying.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_online_cpus.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_possible_cpus.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_enabled_cpus.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_present_cpus.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::num_active_cpus.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_online.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_possible.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_enabled.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_present.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_active.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_dying.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_is_offline","include/linux/cpumask.h::assign_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__assign_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::set_cpu_enabled","include/linux/cpumask.h::set_cpu_present","include/linux/cpumask.h::set_cpu_active","include/linux/cpumask.h::set_cpu_dying","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_all_mask","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_none_mask"],"text":"Global CPU state views expose online enabled present possible active and dying sets through counts predicates and iterators, with a single-CPU configuration that hardwires singleton behavior and a multi-CPU configuration that consults global masks, plus setters that assign one state bit in the corresponding global mask and aliases for the global all and none masks."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/linux/cpumask_types.h::cpumask","include/linux/cpumask_types.h::cpumask_t","include/linux/cpumask_types.h::cpumask_var_t.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask_types.h::cpumask_var_t.alt_2"],"text":"A CPU mask is a fixed-size bitmap object sized by the configured maximum CPU count, with an alias type for the same layout and two variable forms: pointer indirection in one configuration and single-element array embedding in the other."},{"kind":"struct","sids":["include/linux/crash_core.h::crash_mem"],"text":"Crash reservation tracking stores a capacity bound, a used count, and a trailing flexible array of address ranges whose length is tied to the capacity annotation."},{"kind":"export","sids":["include/linux/crash_core.h::arch_kexec_protect_crashkres","include/linux/crash_core.h::arch_kexec_unprotect_crashkres","include/linux/crash_core.h::arch_crash_handle_hotplug_event","include/linux/crash_core.h::arch_crash_hotplug_support","include/linux/crash_core.h::crash_get_elfcorehdr_size"],"text":"Default crash and kexec hooks provide empty protection and hotplug handling with success indication for hotplug support queries and zero size for the ELF core header, suitable for architectures without special handling."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_zero","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_wrap","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_next_and_wrap","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_but","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_and_but","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_andnot_but","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_nth_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_nth_and_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_set_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear_cpus","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_and_set_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_test_and_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::assign_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__assign_cpu"],"text":"Index handling validates CPU numbers before use except that a negative one start is explicitly accepted for successor and exclusion searches to mean before the first entry, while ordinal selectors validate the ordinal and range clearing validates the end of the span."},{"kind":"invariant","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_setall","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_copy","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_size","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_empty","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_full","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_weight_andnot","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_right","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_left","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_random","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_last","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_and","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_or"],"text":"Width selection distinguishes a small scan width for most queries and updates, a live count for fullness and randomized choice, and a large width for clearing copying and sizing, with a fast path for setting all bits when the small width is a compile-time constant."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_BITS_NONE","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_BITS_CPU0","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_BITS_ALL.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_BITS_ALL.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_MASK_ALL.alt_1","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_MASK_ALL.alt_2","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_MASK_NONE","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPU_MASK_CPU0","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_all_mask","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpu_none_mask"],"text":"Static initializer constants provide all-zero, CPU-zero-only, and all-ones mask contents with tail-word masking for the final partial word, exposed both as raw bit-array initializers and as typed mask values, plus aliases for the global all and none masks."},{"kind":"abi_constant","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::CPUMAP_FILE_MAX_BYTES","include/linux/cpumask.h::CPULIST_FILE_MAX_BYTES","include/linux/crash_core.h::ELF_CORE_HEADER_ALIGN","include/linux/cpumask_types.h::__LINUX_CPUMASK_TYPES_H","include/linux/crash_core.h::LINUX_CRASH_CORE_H"],"text":"File size caps bound textual mask and list representations as a function of configured CPUs and page size, the ELF core header alignment is a page-sized power of two, and header guards protect the mask type and crash headers from multiple inclusion."},{"kind":"hazard","sids":["include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_set_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__cpumask_clear_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::__assign_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::assign_cpu","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_local_spread","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_and_distribute","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_any_distribute","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_right","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_shift_left","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_clear","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_copy","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_full","include/linux/cpumask.h::cpumask_setall"],"text":"Relaxed double-underscore modifiers and direct assignment lack the atomicity of their plain counterparts and are unsafe under concurrent updates, distribution and spread helpers do not actually distribute in the shown configuration, and shift copy clear and full operations use differing widths so mixing them without attention can leave tail bits inconsistent."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/asm-generic/bitops/instrumented-atomic.h::clear_bit"],"text":"Cross-dossier symbol referenced by this lane; internals owned elsewhere."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/asm-generic/bitops/instrumented-atomic.h::test_and_clear_bit"],"text":"Cross-dossier symbol referenced by this lane; internals owned elsewhere."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/asm-generic/bitops/instrumented-atomic.h::test_and_set_bit"],"text":"Cross-dossier symbol referenced by this lane; internals owned elsewhere."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/asm-generic/bitsperlong.h::small_const_nbits"],"text":"Cross-dossier symbol referenced by this lane; internals owned elsewhere."},{"kind":"stub","sids":["include/linux/atomic/atomic-arch-fallback.h::raw_atomic_read"],"text":"Cross-dossier symbol referenced by this lane; internals owned elsewhere."},